+
+    <html>
+    <head>
+        <meta charset='utf-8'>
+        <title>Public vs Private GDPR Enforcement Analysis</title>
+        <style>
+            body { font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif; margin: 2rem; line-height: 1.6; }
+            h1, h2, h3 { color: #003366; }
+            blockquote { border-left: 4px solid #5c6f91; padding-left: 1rem; color: #2d3644; }
+            table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
+            th, td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.5rem; }
+            th { background-color: #f0f4fb; }
+        </style>
+    </head>
+    <body>
+        <h1>How Public Bodies Fare Under GDPR Enforcement</h1>
+        <p>This report analyses 999 GDPR enforcement decisions from the cleaned wide table, contrasting how public-sector bodies fare relative to private organisations and other respondent types.</p>
+        <h2>Dataset coverage</h2>
+        <p>The dataset spans 9 jurisdictions (Q1) between 2018 and 2025. Public bodies account for 16.1% of all defendants, with private organisations representing 66.5% and the remainder being natural persons or unspecified entities.</p>
+        <h2>Entity classification</h2>
+        <p>Public-sector defendants appear in 161 decisions, with 18.6% resulting in fines and a median fine of €0. Private organisations face fines in 67.6% of cases (median €5,000), while natural persons rarely receive monetary penalties.</p>
+        <h2>Investigation initiation</h2>
+        <p>Complaint-led triggers (Q15) dominate for both public (67.1%) and private (82.8%) bodies, but DPAs open ex officio investigations nearly twice as often against public entities (11.8% vs 5.1%).</p>
+        <h2>Substantive findings and breach patterns</h2>
+        <p>Security safeguards are cited in 46.6% of public decisions versus 38.1% for private organisations, while lawfulness/transparency violations remain more prevalent for private actors (38.4% vs 23.6%). Accountability issues affect 19.3% of public cases. When breaches are described (Q21), public decisions link to human error (18.0%) and organisational failures (14.9%) slightly more than private matters (11.9% and 7.1%). Rights findings (Q57) emphasise information duties for both groups (26.1% public vs 36.0% private), but public bodies also face access obligations (12.4%) whereas private organisations more often confront erasure shortcomings (10.4%).</p>
+        <h2>Enforcement outcomes</h2>
+        <p>Administrative fines (Q53) issue in 26.1% of public decisions versus 76.8% for private organisations. Public bodies instead receive warnings (33.5% vs 8.1%), reprimands (32.9% vs 17.6%) and compliance orders (33.5% vs 28.8%) more frequently than private actors.</p>
+        <h2>Temporal and jurisdictional evolution</h2>
+        <p>Public-sector enforcement appears in the record from 2018, reaches 13 decisions in 2020, and drops to 1 case by 2024. Ex officio shares decline from 100.0% in 2018 to 0.0% in 2024, signalling greater reliance on complaints over time. The United Kingdom, Italy, Spain, and France contribute the largest clusters of public-body cases, each with contrasting private-sector fine intensities.</p>
+        <h2>Multivariate modelling insights</h2>
+        <p>Controlling for severity proxies, public bodies have a 0.14× lower odds of receiving fines than comparable private organisations. When fines do occur, log-linear models show fine magnitudes aligning once aggravating factors are considered.</p>
+        <h2>Legal practitioner takeaways</h2>
+        <blockquote>Expect DPAs to prioritise corrective orders over monetary sanctions for public bodies, especially where transparency and access duties lapse. Nevertheless, recurring principle violations—particularly around lawful basis—invite sustained oversight, so remediation plans should be documented and promptly executed.</blockquote>
+        <h2>Visual appendix</h2>
+        <figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Time Trends.Png'/><figcaption>Annual counts show how enforcement attention to public sector bodies compares with other defendant types.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Initiation Comparison.Png'/><figcaption>Complaint-driven investigations dominate across the board, but public bodies face a higher mix of ex officio probes.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Article5 Heatmap.Png'/><figcaption>Heatmap reveals which substantive GDPR principles most frequently appear in findings against each entity type.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Rights Heatmap.Png'/><figcaption>Heatmap reveals which substantive GDPR principles most frequently appear in findings against each entity type.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Powers Comparison.Png'/><figcaption>Public-sector decisions rely more heavily on reprimands and compliance orders, while private organizations more often face fines.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src='data:image/png;base64,' alt='Jurisdiction Focus.Png'/><figcaption>Six most active jurisdictions for public bodies also show contrasting private-enforcement footprints.</figcaption></figure>
